-- §4 `gen`: the RESIDUAL of a `requires` discharge is part of the inferred
-- principal context `P'`, so it is deferred into the scheme AND abstracted as a
-- dict parameter.  Issue 1549 (S0), the LEGAL cell.
--
-- HAND-DERIVED FROM THE SPEC, BEFORE THE BINARY WAS ASKED:
--   `Wrap x : Wrap t`, and `tagOf : Tag t => t -> String`, so the body poses the
--   goal `Tag (Wrap t)`.  §3 `inst`: the only matching instance is
--   `impl Tag (Wrap a) requires Tag a`, and discharging it leaves the RESIDUAL
--   `Tag t`.  `t` is generalizable, so §4.2 OD2 defers it to the binder that
--   quantifies it, and §4 `gen` makes it the principal context:
--       nest : Tag a => a -> String
--   `gen` then abstracts one dict parameter per predicate of `P'`
--   (`λ(d₁:π₁)…(dₘ:πₘ)`), so `nest` is emitted with arity 2 (1 dict + 1 value).
--   `main` instantiates at `a = Int`; §4 `var` discharges `Tag Int` against
--   `impl Tag Int`, whose `tagOf` answers "int", so `tagOf (Wrap 5)` is
--   "wrap(" ++ "int" ++ ")".
-- EXPECTED: wrap(int)
--
-- BEFORE THE FIX the residual was dropped entirely: `check` printed
-- `nest : a -> String` at exit 0, `run` printed the right value for the wrong
-- reason (declaration-order fallback), and the BUILT BINARY SEGFAULTED at 139 --
-- the dropped predicate is also the dict parameter that was never abstracted.
-- The section-3 IR row is the assertion that can see that half; no behavioural
-- assertion can, because `run` was already printing "wrap(int)".
data Wrap a = Wrap a

interface Tag t where
  tagOf : t -> String

impl Tag Int where
  tagOf _ = "int"

impl Tag (Wrap a) requires Tag a where
  tagOf (Wrap x) = "wrap(\{tagOf x})"

nest x = tagOf (Wrap x)

main = println (nest 5)
